OSD_signal: no signal delivery at all on wasm32-wasip1.

The largest of the eight files, and the change is one new arm plus one guarded include.

The arm EXTENDS the file's own top-level platform chain: `#ifdef _WIN32 ... #else /* ! _WIN32 */`
becomes `#ifdef _WIN32 ... #elif defined(__wasi__) ... #else /* ! _WIN32 */`. The POSIX branch is
left byte for byte unchanged, and that is the point of the shape. It is four hundred lines of
sigaction() machinery, and the alternative, threading `#ifndef __wasi__` through it, is what PR
#2076 attempted: that patch pasted one twenty-line stub twenty times and rewrote about twenty
unrelated `#endif`s into `#else`, leaving nine "#else after #else" errors and two unterminated
`#if`s, in a file no compiler could preprocess on any platform.

The include is `#include <csignal>`, at the top of the file and outside both platform branches. It
has no condition to extend and gets `#ifndef __wasi__`. wasi-libc's <signal.h> is an #error without
-D_WASI_EMULATED_SIGNAL, and that define is measured (#2172) to leave this file with seventeen
errors rather than zero, because the emulation supplies the SIG* constants and signal() and
supplies no sigset_t, sigaction, sigemptyset, sigaddset or sigprocmask. OCCT installs its handlers
with sigaction() throughout, so the emulation answers none of what this file needs.

The WASI arm defines exactly the five OSD members each of the other two arms defines, no more and
no fewer: SetFloatingSignal, ToCatchFloatingSignals, SetThreadLocalSignal, SetSignal and
ControlBreak.

WHAT THE WASI BRANCH RETURNS:

  * SetFloatingSignal(bool) does nothing. The POSIX branch already does nothing on every platform
    that is neither glibc/Linux nor Solaris, so this matches it, and wasm32 has no floating-point
    exception trapping to enable in any case.
  * ToCatchFloatingSignals() returns false, the same value the POSIX branch returns off glibc, and
    a truthful one: nothing is being caught.
  * SetThreadLocalSignal() forwards to SetFloatingSignal(), which is the POSIX branch's body too.
    Its two in-tree callers, OSD_ThreadPool.cxx:288 and :301, pass OSD::SignalMode() and ignore the
    result.
  * SetSignal() installs nothing and, deliberately, does NOT record theSignalMode in
    OSD_WasSetSignal. OSD::SignalMode() therefore keeps reporting OSD_SignalMode_AsIs, which is the
    truthful answer, and the alternative would be a mode with no handler behind it. That value
    reaches SetThreadLocalSignal(), a no-op here, and Draw_BasicCommands.cxx:952, which this build
    does not compile.
  * ControlBreak() never raises. It exists to turn a delivered SIGINT into an
    OSD_Exception_CTRL_BREAK at a point the algorithm chose; no SIGINT is ever delivered on wasip1,
    so none is ever pending. Callers poll it inside long loops and read "did not raise" as "keep
    going", which is correct here.

NOT AFFECTED: OCC_CATCH_SIGNALS. OCCT's own cmake adds -DOCC_CONVERT_SIGNALS for every non-Windows
target (adm/cmake/occt_defs_flags.cmake:48), so that macro expands to a real setjmp() inside OCCT
here as it does elsewhere. It is lowered by -mllvm -wasm-enable-sjlj and is independent of this
file, which never installs the handler the signal-conversion path would have needed.

Issue: https://github.com/SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift/issues/2173

diff --git a/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/OSD/OSD_signal.cxx b/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/OSD/OSD_signal.cxx
index 66c6002d..9d5b122d 100644
--- a/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/OSD/OSD_signal.cxx
+++ b/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/OSD/OSD_signal.cxx
@@ -21,7 +21,9 @@
 
 #include <atomic>
 #include <mutex>
-#include <csignal>
+#ifndef __wasi__
+  #include <csignal>
+#endif
 
 #include <Standard_WarningDisableFunctionCast.hxx>
 
@@ -723,6 +725,53 @@ LONG _osd_debug(void)
 } // end _osd_debug
   #endif /* ! OCCT_UWP && ! __CYGWIN__ && ! __MINGW32__ */
 
+#elif defined(__wasi__)
+
+//---------- wasm32-wasip1 : no signal delivery at all ------------------------
+
+// wasi-libc's <signal.h> is an #error without -D_WASI_EMULATED_SIGNAL, and the emulation that
+// define unlocks supplies the SIG* constants and signal() while supplying no sigset_t,
+// sigaction, sigemptyset, sigaddset or sigprocmask, which is what the POSIX branch below installs
+// its handlers with. A wasip1 instance receives no signals, so there is nothing to install.
+
+//=================================================================================================
+
+void OSD::SetFloatingSignal(bool theFloatingSignal)
+{
+  (void)theFloatingSignal;
+}
+
+//=================================================================================================
+
+bool OSD::ToCatchFloatingSignals()
+{
+  return false;
+}
+
+//=================================================================================================
+
+void OSD::SetThreadLocalSignal(OSD_SignalMode /*theSignalMode*/, bool theFloatingSignal)
+{
+  SetFloatingSignal(theFloatingSignal);
+}
+
+//=================================================================================================
+
+void OSD::SetSignal(OSD_SignalMode theSignalMode, bool theFloatingSignal)
+{
+  SetFloatingSignal(theFloatingSignal);
+  (void)theSignalMode;
+  // OSD_WasSetSignal is deliberately left at OSD_SignalMode_AsIs: nothing was installed, and
+  // OSD::SignalMode() reporting a mode that was set would be a claim no handler backs.
+}
+
+//=================================================================================================
+
+void OSD::ControlBreak()
+{
+  // no SIGINT is ever delivered, so no interrupt can be pending
+}
+
 #else /* ! _WIN32 */
 
 //---------- All Systems except Windows NT : ----------------------------------
